<p>
    The non-player characters in {{ externalLink("https://store.steampowered.com/app/489830/The_Elder_Scrolls_V_Skyrim_Special_Edition/", "Skyrim") }}
    tell their stories in two different ways.
    The first is traditional dialogue, where they talk to the player directly.
    But when you delve into a dungeon alone, there is nobody to talk to.
    In dungeons, characters communicate with the player a second way:
    through letters, diaries, notes, and environmental clues that can be found lying about.
</p>
<p>
    Skyrim rarely takes control away from the player,
    so in both forms of writing, the player must be an active participant, as if they are a detective trying to solve the story.
    Dialogue puts the player in the role of an interrogator,
    while environmental storytelling puts the player in the role of a crime scene investigator.
    Which detective role is more exciting?
</p>
<p>
    The difference between the two forms of writing in Skyrim
    is the biggest difference between <a href="/epiphanylist/talosprinciple">The Talos Principle</a> and its sequel.
    The original Talos relies mostly on environmental storytelling because there is nobody to talk to,
    while the sequel is full of dialogue, and there are 999 robots to talk to.
    {%- call sidenote("sn-smalltribe") %}
    I find myself jealous of the 1000-robot version of social media.
    The real internet is big and scary.
    {% endcall %}
</p>
<p>
    In addition to using a different writing delivery mechanism, the writing itself takes a more straightforward approach.
    The story of the original Talos Principle revolved around the mystery of who and where you were.
    <em>The Talos Principle 2</em>, by contrast, starts off with an hour-long info dump about who and where you are,
    as it elaborates on the world that was implied at the end of the first game.
    The result is more thorough, but less mysterious.
</p>
<p>
    In fact, the story of <em>The Talos Principle 2</em> loses enough mystery compared to the original
    that I do not consider it an <a href="/epiphanylist">Epiphany Game</a>.
    It is a more typical dialogue-driven story, and the plot is largely explicit rather than discovered.
</p>
<p>
    But, <em>The Talos Principle 2</em> is still a fun puzzle game.
    It retains the same core as Talos 1, while getting rid of all timing-based mechanics. 
    You no longer have to run past roving bombs, or record yourself doing a precise sequence of movements.
    The puzzles are thus more like {{ externalLink("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite-state_machine", "finite-state machines") -}}.
    There are about a dozen new gadgets to play with.
    My favorite mechanic in the original was laser connections, so I was happy to see four new gadgets that interact with lasers.
</p>
<p>
    Although the new mechanics add complexity, the game does not push the puzzle difficulty to its full, sadistic potential.
    Actually, I thought most of the game was too easy. I wish the whole game was the same level as the 12 "gold" puzzles.
    {%- call sidenote("sn-difficulty") %}
    I have heard that the <em>Road to Elysium</em> DLC is more challenging,
    but I have not yet played it.
    {% endcall %}
    All of the new tetromino puzzles felt like a waste of time, as they were easily solved through brute-force random placement.
    I never 'chieved this achievement:
</p>
{{ articleImage(
    "/epiphanylist/pageassets/talosprinciple2-chief.png",
