<p>
    The level progression in Talos 2 is extremely formulaic.
    There are 12 worlds, each with one new mechanic, 11 puzzles, 2 collectible stars, and a lore drop.
    The stars themselves are also formulaic: They are easily findable via quest marker, and they are each obtained by completing one of three types of puzzle.
    I was disappointed by this change.
    Finding all the stars was my favorite part of Talos 1, because the locations were highly varied and they required creative, outside-the-box solutions.
    But in Talos 2, the stars felt like a repetitive, completionist chore.
</p>
<p>
    The environments in <em>The Talos Principle 2</em> are beautiful,
    {%- call sidenote("sn-graphics") %}
    Unfortunately, I am the type of person to turn my graphics settings to low, because it's easier to see the important objects on low.
    Sorry, <em>Talos Principle 2</em>, I didn't properly appreciate your beauty.
    {% endcall %}
    and absolutely <strong>massive.</strong>
    They are so big that even given a fast run speed, it can take over a minute to walk between puzzles.
    I appreciate the ambition, but it feels a bit like if I were to start a big cross-stitch without realizing how much work it was going to be.
    Only a small percentage of the world has meaningful content.
    I am not sure whether it's a good thing or bad thing that the game provides quest markers for all important locations.
    On one hand, it neuters the exploration element.
    On the other hand, exploring such a huge world would be extremely tedious.
</p>
<ul class="proconlist">
    <li class="positive">New puzzle mechanics are fun</li>
    <li class="positive">Beautiful environments and structures</li>
    <li class="subjective">More dialogue and cinematics than the first entry</li>
    <li class="negative">Formulaic and predictable, all 12 worlds play the same</li>
    <li class="negative">World is too big and it takes forever to move between puzzles</li>
</ul>
